--TEST--
Core\IO::isReadable and ::isWritable answer the operating system's question about a file and a directory that are there, and answer `false` rather than throwing for a name that is not — the bound named on both sides, beside the `exists` that agrees about the same absent name — spec § 14, ADR 0118 § 2
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Both members are guards rather than gates: what they answer is a snapshot of
// a permission anything may change, so the shape a program reaches for is
// still the operation inside a `try`. What they are for is the case where
// "cannot" is an ordinary branch -- a config file that may be absent, a
// directory a tool offers to use if it can.
Core\IO::write("note.txt", "hello\n");
Core\IO::makeDir("sub");

if (Core\IO::isReadable("note.txt")) {
    echo "note.txt: readable\n";
} else {
    echo "note.txt: not readable\n";
}
if (Core\IO::isWritable("note.txt")) {
    echo "note.txt: writable\n";
} else {
    echo "note.txt: not writable\n";
}

// A directory is a path like any other here. Neither member is about the kind
// of thing at the name -- that is `isFile` and `isDir` -- and a program that
// wants to know whether it may create a file asks about the directory, since
// the name it means does not exist yet.
if (Core\IO::isReadable("sub")) {
    echo "sub: readable\n";
} else {
    echo "sub: not readable\n";
}

// The other side. A name that is not there is not readable and not writable,
// which is an answer and not a failure: `size` throws for the same name
// because it has no number to give, while "can this be read" has a perfectly
// good `false`.
if (Core\IO::exists("missing.txt")) {
    echo "missing.txt: exists\n";
} else {
    echo "missing.txt: absent\n";
}
if (Core\IO::isReadable("missing.txt")) {
    echo "missing.txt: readable\n";
} else {
    echo "missing.txt: not readable\n";
}
if (Core\IO::isWritable("missing.txt")) {
    echo "missing.txt: writable\n";
} else {
    echo "missing.txt: not writable\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
write = ["."]
--EXPECT--
note.txt: readable
note.txt: writable
sub: readable
missing.txt: absent
missing.txt: not readable
missing.txt: not writable
